WHY THE ATTACHMENT
Statements arrive by email far more often than they get uploaded by hand, and
the ingestion path was extracting the figures and discarding the document. The
tax pack then shipped a rent line with nothing supporting it — the exact gap this
area of the product exists to close. Deliberately left out of the previous track
as out of scope; requested directly here.

Same bucket and path shape as app/api/rental-statements/extract/route.ts, so
both paths produce interchangeable rows. No migration needed: 064 already
enumerates rental_payments.statement_path in both storage-object functions.

WHY THE EXTRACTOR CHANGED — the substance of this commit
Measured, not assumed. Three runs of the email path over the SAME statement,
same code:

  run 1: other_outgoings absent            (want 2146)
  run 2: other_outgoings 2146 ✓, other_income 760.20 ✗
  run 3: other_outgoings 3148 ✗, other_income 760.20 ✗, confidence 1.0

The manual upload route, on the same PDFs, was exact on every field across both
statements. The difference is mechanism: the email parser OCRs the attachment to
text and asks for freeform JSON with no schema; extractRentalStatementFields
sends the native PDF through generateObject with a Zod schema, per-field
descriptions and no coercion.

Two things made this worse than ordinary flakiness. Confidence 1.0 while wrong
means confidence cannot be used to filter it. And other_income repeatedly
received the amount DISBURSED to the owner, which feeds the ATO "Other
rental-related income" line — reporting $760.20 of income that does not exist,
on top of the rent.

other_income and other_income_note are therefore taken ONLY from the structured
extraction when one ran — `fields ? fields.other_income : parsed.otherIncome`,
not `??` — because the email parser's value there is actively wrong rather than
merely absent, and `??` would let a null structured answer fall back to it.

The email parser still decides rental-vs-expense and matches the property. It
just no longer supplies the money.

`extracted` records `figures_from: "statement_pdf" | "email_text"` and the full
structured result, so a confirmed figure can always be traced to its origin.

A TYPES GAP FOUND
email_ingestion_log has existed since migration 045 but was never added to the
hand-maintained database.types.ts, so any typed query against it failed to
compile. The handler uses an untyped SupabaseClient, which is why nobody noticed.
Added rather than cast around, per the style guide.

